# TUI-DESIGN-5 §2.6 / §2.7 / §10 (slot R5-2): `/pause`, `/pause now` and `/end` with its confirm ladder under a
# real pty, at 80x24 AND 40x24. Hermetic: `--mock` only — no ledger, no second process, no network, no key.
#
# STATUS — this file does not assert its §2.6/§2.7 ANSWERS yet, and that is a dependency, not a skip.
#   The registry rows, the `[now] [<target>]` grammar, §12 S45a/S45b and the four-rung `/end` ladder are landed
#   (R5-2, `src/tui/commands/{registry,dispatch,confirm}.ts`), but the HANDLERS that call `engine.pause(opts)` /
#   `engine.end(opts)` and write the `session:end` index line are R5-1's `src/cli/session.ts` arms (D-AS's one
#   `INDEX_KINDS` commit is W4). Until they land, `send /end` resolves and refuses at the host rather than ending,
#   so the marks below capture the ROWS and the ladder, which is what §2.13's 40- and 80-column cells pin.
#   Committed now, with its rows, so the integrator has the scenario the day the handlers resolve.
#
# run-smoke.sh needs three rows for this scenario; R5-2 owns `test/pty/**` and lands them in W5:
#   env    : r5-pause-end) ;;
#   check  : r5-pause-end) grep -q 'needs a live run' "$txt" && checks="$checks r5-pause-end:s45a" || { ok=0; checks="$checks MISSING:r5-pause-end-s45a"; }
#   select : sel_named r5-pause-end && run r5-pause-end 0 24 80 chat --mock --mode jev-on
#
# What it proves, beyond "it renders":
#   - §12 S45a: `/pause` with no target and no live run is the HAND-WRITTEN sentence, never the generated
#     `error: /pause needs a live run` the `'live'` availability row used to produce (§14.2 #16, #39);
#   - §2.7 / §14.2 #39: `/end` from an IDLE session with a committed step does NOT refuse — it ends the session;
#     only a session with no run at all answers S45b;
#   - §2.6: `/pause now` is the soft interrupt on a live run and the status word is CO §7.6's `pausing · …`;
#   - §2.13's two cells at 40 columns: the `/end` ladder falls to `y end · Y now · n stay` and stays readable;
#   - TD4 D-X §4.5: Enter on the confirm row is inert — the run is still live after it;
#   - G-R5-9 / §7 row 61: `assertNoKeyBytes` over every frame.

expect \x1b\[\?25l
expect Say hi
sleep 0.4

# 1 — §12 S45a at 80x24: `/pause` with nothing running. The sentence names the targeted form, so the refusal is
#     also the documentation of the verb that WOULD have worked.
send /pause
sleep 0.2
send \r
expect needs a live run
sleep 0.3
mark pause-no-run-80

# 2 — §2.13 at 40 columns: the same refusal, cut to the width, never wrapped by Ink (§2.1)
resize 24 40
sleep 0.8
send /pause
sleep 0.2
send \r
sleep 0.4
mark pause-no-run-40

# 3 — a live run, so the local form has something to pause
resize 24 80
sleep 0.6
send do a thing
sleep 0.2
send \r
sleep 1.2
mark run-live

# 4 — §2.6 / CO §7.6: `/pause` at the step boundary. The status word says what is being waited for and how to
#     escalate, so the wait is never one the user cannot get out of.
send /pause
sleep 0.2
send \r
sleep 0.6
mark pausing

# 5 — the run settles paused; §12 S19's `paused after step <n>` names the way forward
sleep 1.2
mark paused

# 6 — §2.7 / §14.2 #39: `/end` from a session whose run is no longer LIVE. This is the case the first
#     implementation refused (`KeyRunPhase` has no `paused` member, so a paused session reports `run: 'none'`),
#     which made the ordinary pause-then-end flow unreachable.
send /end
sleep 0.2
send \r
sleep 0.5
mark end-after-pause

# 7 — §2.13 at 40 columns: the ladder's narrowest rung. `y end · Y now · n stay` is 22 cells, so all three
#     answers survive a 40-column card — a modal must never lose its way out.
resize 24 40
sleep 0.8
mark end-40

send /exit
sleep 0.2
send \r
eof
